o controls  its own currency so there's been a lot  of readjustment about how we should look  at debt especially for countries that  control their own currency and the us is  the world's biggest economy and when  we're talking about the politics of  driving up the debt i mean this is a  bipartisan  disaster if you will  yeah you mentioned the trump tax cuts  there's no doubt about that uh democrats  in recent years because of the pandemic  they would say have been spending quite  heavily republicans would counter that  they've gone beyond their mandate to  improve the economy they've done a  little too much social spending uh  interestingly if you look back you know  back to let's say right after the reagan  years where there was a big deficit that  greeted george h.w bush upon his arrival  he you know went uh to the democrats and  together they created concessions they  created compromises and um he raised  taxes despite promising not to do that  um and they managed to work out that  deficit and bring the debt down a little  bit and um that was something that we  actually haven't really seen in the same  measure in decades since you know for  conservatives uh tax hikes uh that's  anathema that's something that is not  politically possible george h.w h.w bush  lost the next election right we should  mention because he was pummeled for  going back on that promise  no new taxes right um and at the same  time you know democrats are very  hesitant to hesitate to rein in spending  on social services the ones that the  republicans often pummel whether it's  social security whether it's medicare  medicaid so there is this reluctance  we've seen that kind of division in  recent years and it we see it here with  the debt levels as well and we know  people are complaining about the prices  they see at the grocery store now  inflation now at rates we haven't seen  since the early 80s the u.s fed says it  wants to start raising interest rates as  early as next  month what is that going to mean for the  debt  yeah i mean honestly inflation actually  would help pay off the debt more but it  would be terrible for people so no one  really is going to pursue that route we  want to pull down inflation  interest rates will also increase the  amount of debt service right that will  make that interest uh that that interest  more expensive to pay off  so the question is you know how are you  going to do that what's going to happen  now some would say that as long as u.s  growth is well above the interest rates  then naturally things are going to come  down the only problem is that really  that still taxes those who have assets  for example those who are saving so in a  way the country pays off its debt  through attacks on those on the wealth  of normal americans so you know there's  criticisms of both side you know it's  not a one-to-one ratio that if the  country is growing more than the  interest rates that it's going to be  able to pay that off so there there is  push back on this idea let me ask you  about the other big economic story today  and that is the great resignation it's  not slowing down we got numbers out um  today for december more than four  million americans quit their jobs um in  december for 2021 a total of more than  40 million people said take this job and  shove it that's never happened in the us  before either i mean the u.s is a very  flexible job market it's very different  from here in germany or in europe for  example so people have always left and  come back to jobs very easily but yeah  we're seeing something that is very  different than normal um you know what  we saw was a lot of people who left they  had to work at home they maybe left  precarious jobs jobs they didn't like  kovid shook things up and now they're  not coming back we have to remember  there is still a virus in the u.s so  there are some people who don't want to  come back maybe afraid of coming back  but there obviously has been a  reevaluation by others there are plenty  who maybe on the margins they found that  they could afford to not have to work  maybe they were close to retirement  maybe they weren't the breadwinner in  their family and they found that they  didn't actually have to be at work um or  maybe there are others who wanted to try  something different they found that  coveted gave them a chance to reset and  they weren't scared to maybe go in a  different path so it's hard to say what  exactly is going on but with the  flexible job market like you have in the  u.s one that's so fluid  it is easier to come back so we still  might see something happen  especially after you know this virus is  finally when that happens if that  happens and we know that teachers have  been quitting in alarming numbers and  also i can imagine that a lot of people  who have been working from home  have discovered like like you were just  describing you know why do i need to sit  in traffic jams anymore right i mean  it's the work-life balance that thanks  to the coronavirus now i've realized i  want to live better than i did before  yeah for sure i mean um you know  recruiters for example have been saying  in recent years that more and more  younger generations of workers more  younger workers in these younger  generations their value assessment when  it comes to work and what they want out  of it is shifting fundamentally it's not  as much about the paycheck it's not as  much about the position but it's about  the work-life balance as you said and  covet has given workers even more reason  to see firsthand what it's like if they  spend more time at home if they don't  have to sit in traffic commutes are a  big thing in the u.s as both you and i  know so that's no small thing when it  comes to quality of life so if workers  are preferring better quality of life  then they're giving a total reassessment  in general to what they may be doing as  their profession makes you feel lucky  when you can take a bike to work  abso